  "body": "**Status: tooling improvement (verification engineering). No new \u03ba. No mathematical check is changed, skipped or weakened, and no pinned file or orchestrator is edited.**\n\nTrack 85\u201394, OS-random seed **87**. Base: current main `ce7ad1b6`. The work started on `29218749`, and every pinned input is unchanged between the two. Only `research/fast-transport-audit-seed87/` is added.\n\n## What it speeds up\n\nPR635's `hpe_audit_and_transport.run` (sha `c9a05ff2\u2026`) is the per-trial cost of every negative-control suite in the cold chain. Each suite runs 96 full audits:\n\n- T37 layer-end, 77 s (recorded cold-chain time);\n- #799 layer-end, 151 s;\n- #741 p6n layer-end, 114 s; layered66, 64.5 s; inherited cold-end, 114 s;\n- the five `layer_end_controls_leaver.py` runs that the selected #1094 lineage rebuilds.\n\n`fast_controls.py` is a drop-in for `python3 <control script> <args>`. An import hook lets the script select `hpe_audit_and_transport.py` by its own rule and executes it from the hashed bytes, never a `.pyc`. Only for the validated sha256 does it rebind five exact primitives:\n\n- `Q`: integral values as `int`;\n- `apply`: integer and \u00b11 fast paths, with the same assertion order, lazy row access and zero-dropping;\n- `copy`: structural copy of flat gate records only;\n- `basis`: memoised on all-`int` inputs;\n- `state_maps`: scaled-integer dyadic P and P\u207b\u00b9, falling back to the pinned code for anything else.\n\n`run()` and every assertion and message remain the pinned code objects. An unvalidated module runs unaccelerated, or exits non-zero under `--require-fast`.\n\n## Evidence\n\n```sh\npython3 -I -B research/fast-transport-audit-seed87/verify.py            # tests + 4 real suites (~10 min)\npython3 -I -B research/fast-transport-audit-seed87/verify.py --quick    # tests + T37 suite (~2.5 min)\n```\n\nExpected final line: `PASS_FAST_HPE_TRANSPORT suites=4 audits=384 identical_receipts_logs_outcomes cpu=\u2026`. Deterministic fields are compared with `EXPECTED.json`. An independent `--quick` rerun and a clean sparse clone of this branch both reproduced it.\n\n- **Four archived suites, 384 audits.** Inputs, receipts and logs are read from the repo's own evidence tarballs. Each unchanged script runs directly, through `--pinned`, and through `--require-fast`:\n  - `COLD-END-CONTROLS.json` is byte-identical across the three runs and matches the archived receipt apart from its absolute `word` path;\n  - stdout is identical across the three runs and equal to the archived log for the three suites that have one, apart from the seconds line;\n  - all 96 per-audit outcomes are identical (exception type, `repr(args)` and `run()` line, or receipt text plus both output-word hashes).\n- **Differential tests, under both Fraction constructors.** Each primitive is compared with the pinned function on value, key order and exception type and message:\n  - `apply`: 4,000 programs, including malformed gates;\n  - `state_maps`: 300 programs (227 take the dyadic path) and the real 12,913-gate prefix;\n  - `basis`: 3,000 inputs, including non-int and cached calls;\n  - the copy, at nesting depths 1 to 2,000;\n  - `run()` on two module instances, for the honest word plus four deep-nesting variants;\n  - the import hook (it selects the script's own sibling over `$PR635_CODE`) and the hash guard.\n\n  Negative controls: a sabotaged `apply` is detected, and a zero-coefficient gate matches the pinned code.\n- **Current frontier.** On the #1094 lineage's final k8 word, `layer_end_controls_leaver.py` produced a byte-identical receipt (95 rejected plus 2 declared not-applicable substitutes) and identical stdout; user CPU fell 50.6 \u2192 24.6 s. This was recorded manually because that word isn't archived.\n\n## Adversarial review\n\nThree reviewer subagents tried to break the claim: a primitive fuzzer (~600k `apply` gates), a word mutator (201 new corrupted U8 words, run in fresh processes), and a harness reviewer. They reproduced one real divergence: an earlier copy used one frame per nesting level, so a gate nested about 500\u20131,000 deep made the pinned audit raise `RecursionError` while the accelerated one passed. The copy now handles only flat records.\n\nThey also found that pre-importing from `$PR635_CODE` could substitute a different module for scripts that import a sibling; about 17 directories hold another version. That is why the import hook now leaves the choice to the script. Smaller fixes:\n\n- bare asserts are now distinguished by the `run()` line;\n- the receipt is compared as text;\n- a `.pyc` can no longer bypass the hash guard;\n- the wrapper's own directory is removed from `sys.path`;\n- `--` is passed through to the script.\n\nEach of these cases is now a test.\n\n## Measurements\n\nmacOS arm64, CPython 3.14.8, concurrent load 8\u201322:\n\n| measurement | direct | accelerated | ratio |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| honest U8 audit, best-of-9 process CPU | 0.766 s | 0.334 s | **2.29\u00d7** |\n| four archived suites, CPU | 239.3 s | 106.0 s | **2.26\u00d7** |\n| #1094-lineage leaver suite, user CPU | 50.6 s | 24.6 s | 2.06\u00d7 |\n\nCumulative ablation: Q/apply 1.72\u00d7, +copy 1.72\u00d7, +basis 2.07\u00d7, +state_maps 2.29\u00d7. The copy step is roughly neutral now and can be dropped.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Composes multiplicatively with #1059 (process parallelism; its workers would install the hook) and with #1101 (stage overlap). Not wired into any orchestrator.\n- The layered66 suite (`7829187b\u2026`, input not archived) was not run.\n- Exactness is claimed for `run()`. Helpers return `int` after installation, and behaviour within one frame of the recursion limit is not claimed identical.\n- How this was chosen: five explorer subagents prototyped alternatives (exact audit arithmetic, cross-mutant prefix memo, bit589 dependency portability, T37 emit, open sweep), and three lens judges ranked them. The cross-mutant memo (about 2\u00d7 more CPU, exact) composes with this and is left as a follow-up.\n\n## Credit\n\n- PR635 authors (icekylinx) for the audit.\n- DreamingOfClouds / LayerReviewer for the control scripts.\n- #869/#870, #741, #942, #1005 and #1094 for the archived and rebuilt replays.\n- #1059 for the complementary parallel runner.\n\nPrepared by Sagar Thakrar with substantial assistance from Anthropic Claude (Claude Code), including the explorer, judge and reviewer subagents.\n",
